Post-Operative Arthrofibrosis
Musculoskeletal & Pain
Progressive post-operative stiffness, Firm end-feel on passive motion, Unrelenting deep joint ache

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MKP-045
Reason for Visit: Severe, unrelenting deep knee pain and an acutely locked, stiff joint 8 weeks post-operation, causing extreme distress and inability to engage in physical therapy.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a patient 8 weeks post-total knee replacement experiencing a sudden, severe pain crisis and extreme mechanical stiffness, preventing any movement. Urgent clinical assessment was necessary to differentiate developing severe arthrofibrosis from a catastrophic delayed prosthetic joint infection or hardware failure, and to provide immediate, potent analgesia to break the severe pain-guarding cycle before morning.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My new knee is locked solid; it feels like it is encased in cement, and the deep, throbbing ache is completely unmanageable."
History of Present Illness: Patient is 8 weeks post-uncomplicated right total knee arthroplasty (TKA). Presents with an acute pain and stiffness crisis. Timeline: Failed to achieve expected milestones in physical therapy over the past 4 weeks; developed severe, progressive, and sustained restriction of both active and passive joint range of motion. Today, the patient experienced an unrelenting, deep, aching pain localized to the operated joint that spiked severely after attempting basic home exercises. Patient reports a total lack of sustained functional progress despite strict adherence to the rehabilitation protocol. Any aggressive passive manipulation by the therapist earlier today resulted in intense, agonizing muscle guarding and a flare of the deep ache.
Functional Status: Severely impaired; walking with a stiff, peg-leg gait; completely unable to flex the knee past 40 degrees.
Social/Safety Concerns: Profound psychological distress and despair regarding the failure of the surgical recovery.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 148/92 | HR: 95 | Temp: 36.9 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 88 Kg
General: Alert, visibly distressed, holding the right leg perfectly rigid in slight flexion.
Physical Exam: Inspection of the right knee reveals a well-healed, clean surgical incision with no dehiscence or purulent discharge. The joint exhibits chronic, low-grade warmth and swelling persisting beyond the normal acute postoperative phase, but is not intensely erythematous. Crucial finding: Range of motion is severely restricted (10 degrees extension lag to 40 degrees maximal flexion). Attempting to passively stretch the joint to its extremes encounters a firm, unyielding, hard mechanical end-feel. Surrounding soft tissues are palpable as densely thickened, rigid, and tight. Neurological and vascular status distal to the knee is entirely intact.
Environmental Observations: Exercise equipment (stationary pedal) sits unused due to the severe mechanical block.
Medication Reconciliation: Taking prescribed Paracetamol and Meloxicam with zero relief of the deep fibrotic ache. Refusing opioids due to nausea.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Severe Post-Operative Arthrofibrosis (Right Knee) - Status: Acute Pain Crisis
Differentials:

Prosthetic Joint Infection (PJI) - Ruled out clinically by the absence of systemic fever, lack of intense, spreading erythema, clean surgical wound, and a sub-acute progressive stiffening rather than a hyperacute septic presentation (though inflammatory markers ESR/CRP are mandatory to completely exclude).

Hardware Loosening / Failure - Ruled out clinically by the firm, restricted, stiff nature of the joint rather than an unstable, painful "clunking" or giving way under load.

Complex Regional Pain Syndrome (CRPS) - Ruled out by the absence of severe allodynia, gross vasomotor/skin color changes, and the primary issue being mechanical fibrotic stiffness rather than purely neuropathic hypersensitivity.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented in a severe pain and functional crisis driven by aggressive scar tissue formation (arthrofibrosis) following a knee replacement. The critical clinical imperative was to firmly differentiate this severe mechanical stiffness from a catastrophic delayed joint infection. Clinical examination confirmed the hallmark "hard end-feel" of fibrotic restriction and the absence of acute septic red flags. The severity of the pain and the patient's profound despair rendered them incapable of further rehabilitation. Postponing evaluation would have resulted in unmanaged severe pain and escalating psychological distress. Urgent after-hours assessment provided crucial diagnostic validation (confirming it is scar tissue, not an infection), prevented further counterproductive forceful manipulation, and initiated a new, targeted pharmacological strategy.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered Intramuscular Ketorolac 30mg STAT to rapidly suppress the intense intra-articular inflammation causing the deep ache. Prescribed oral Pregabalin 75mg Nocté to target the hyper-sensitized pain pathways that develop in chronic fibrotic joints. Advised absolute cessation of forceful, agonizing passive stretching.
Positive Behavioral Change:
Action: Discontinue the painful, forceful pushing exercises; instead, perform gentle, sustained (5-minute) pain-free hangs with the heel propped on a rolled towel. Consequence: The gentle, constant gravity stretch encourages the dense scar tissue to slowly yield without triggering the agonizing, defensive muscle spasms caused by aggressive pushing (positive reinforcement).
Action: Apply an ice pack wrapped in a towel for 20 minutes specifically after the gravity stretches. Consequence: Immediate constriction of the inflamed joint capsule, extinguishing the deep, throbbing "cement-like" ache and allowing for a period of comfortable rest (negative reinforcement).
Home-Service (Optional): Urgent communication sent to the operating orthopedic surgeon requesting an early review for potential Manipulation Under Anesthesia (MUA) or arthroscopic lysis of adhesions.
DME/Safety (Optional): N/A
Follow-up: Urgent orthopedic surgeon review within 7 days.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if the knee suddenly becomes intensely hot, bright red, and massively swollen, if pus begins leaking from the old scar, or if a high fever develops.

